3.1 Enterprise use cases:

Use case 1: File processing

    When a customer uploads a 500 MB file to S3, S3 triggers a Lambda function after
    the upload completes.

    Lambda can validate file metadata, initiate downstream processing,
    and update file status.

    For large-file processing, use S3 multipart upload and let Lambda orchestrate
    or delegate processing rather than loading the entire file into memory.


Use case 2: Background processing

    An e-commerce application receives an order.

    The Order Service puts an event into SQS. Lambda consumes the message
    and performs independent work such as sending an order confirmation
    or generating an invoice.


Use case 3: Serverless REST APIs

    A mobile application calls an API Gateway endpoint.

    API Gateway invokes Lambda, which validates the request,
    executes business logic, accesses DynamoDB, and returns a response.

    Useful for lightweight APIs and unpredictable workloads.


Use case 4: Scheduled jobs/cron job

    Suppose an e-commerce company needs to expire pending orders after 30 minutes.

    EventBridge can trigger a Lambda function periodically to identify
    and expire eligible orders.

    For high-volume order expiry, a delayed-message or scheduled-workflow
    architecture may be more appropriate than repeatedly scanning the database.


Use case 5: Database event processing

    When DynamoDB records change, DynamoDB Streams can trigger Lambda.

    Example: When an order status changes from CREATED to SHIPPED,
    Lambda updates a search index or publishes a notification event.


Use case 6: Infrastructure automation

When an infrastructure event occurs, EventBridge can trigger Lambda to perform
automated actions,such as cleaning up unused resources or processing CloudTrail events.


