8.1 Lambda concurrency
    Suppose your Lambda function receives 1,000 requests simultaneously.

    Lambda can create multiple execution environments to handle concurrent invocations.

Concurrency means:

    Number of Lambda invocations executing at the same time.

    Formula:
    \[
    Concurrency = Request\ Rate \times Average\ Duration
    \]

    Example:
    - Incoming requests = 100 per second.
    - Average execution time = 2 seconds.
    \[
    Concurrency = 100 \times 2 = 200
    \]

    You need approximately 200 concurrent executions to handle this workload without
    building up a queue, assuming a steady arrival rate.

    There are three important concepts.

A. Reserved concurrency

    You reserve capacity for a particular function and also establish its maximum
    concurrency.

    Example:
    OrderNotificationLambda

    Reserved concurrency = 100


    This function can use up to 100 concurrent executions.

Why is it useful?

    Suppose you have five Lambda functions, and one of them suddenly receives
    massive traffic.

    Without concurrency controls, that function might consume available
    account concurrency and affect other functions.

    Reserved concurrency protects other workloads by limiting this function.

B. Provisioned concurrency

    AWS initializes a specified number of execution environments in advance.

    Example:
    PaymentValidationLambda

    Provisioned concurrency = 20


Why?

    Suppose a payment API requires low and predictable startup latency.

    Provisioned concurrency keeps 20 environments initialized and
    ready to process requests.

    It costs extra.

C. On-demand concurrency

    AWS creates execution environments as demand increases, subject to concurrency
    quotas and scaling limits.

    Type	Purpose
    On-demand	Automatic scaling
    Reserved	Reserve and cap concurrency
    Provisioned	Pre-initialize environments

